{"chapter": "sites-cohomology", "node_kind": "proof", "parent_tag": "079X", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$j$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{sites-lemma-localize-topos}", "REF_0002": "\\ref{sites-lemma-localize-topos-site}", "REF_0003": "\\ref{lemma-cohomology-of-open}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{proof}\n", "suffix": "\\end{proof}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:1f3b8144e752d23826950d22937ea376cf428d85c0877eee4198d32536dff478", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:fefc0895ccb755bdc02d5e29f0a24378d93e5b09f89111fa2dd135d38b8f8b17", "source_text": "By\nSites, Lemmas <REF_0001> and\n<REF_0002>\nthe morphism of topoi <MATH_0001> is\nequivalent to a localization. Hence this follows from\nLemma <REF_0003>.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:2a5fab08160bf50d8cf6ffcfaf1ab9ec0f03b6b26c9832eaf856729b7d90464d", "unit_id": "tag:07A0:proof"}
{"chapter": "sites-cohomology", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "079X", "placeholders": {"REF_0001": "\\ref{lemma-compute-cohomology-on-sheaf-sets}", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:1f9a85d80009cf5959cc40b287987245fd5a6d5dde9d7cd7a4f5b4c1b7719740", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nKeeping in mind Lemma <REF_0001>\nwe see that the following definition is the ``correct one'' also\nfor sheaves of modules on ringed sites.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:d492a3fc76171fee302cf3f0550b227c9c347d05b45d9650bc357f1011f5bc68", "unit_id": "tag:079X:prose-0002"}
{"chapter": "sites-cohomology", "node_kind": "definition", "parent_tag": "079X", "placeholders": {"FOOTNOTECLOSE_0001": "}", "FOOTNOTEOPEN_0001": "\\footnote{", "MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0002": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0003": "$K$", "MATH_0004": "$H^p(K, \\mathcal{F}) = 0$", "MATH_0005": "$p \\geq 1$", "REF_0001": "\\cite[Vbis, Proposition 1.3.10]{SGA4}", "REF_0002": "\\cite[V, Definition 4.1]{SGA4}", "TEXTCLOSE_0001": "}", "TEXTITCLOSE_0001": "}", "TEXTITOPEN_0001": "{\\it ", "TEXTOPEN_0001": "\\href{mailto:stacks.project@gmail.com}{"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{definition}\n\\label{definition-limp}\n", "suffix": "\\end{definition}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:ad45ea5f374e153e69b91ef23e5800189a6eeca59b6051fdb8f4af2ea9099f90", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:82e066a0078f3c161668792b207273d668aac08e5f2b1f3daee8b81ddbb764e0", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> be a site.\nWe say an abelian sheaf <MATH_0002> is\n<TEXTITOPEN_0001>totally acyclic<TEXTITCLOSE_0001><FOOTNOTEOPEN_0001>Although this terminology is is used in\n<REF_0001> this is probably nonstandard notation.\nIn <REF_0002> this property is dubbed ``flasque'', but\nwe cannot use this because it would clash with our definition\nof flasque sheaves on topological spaces. Please email\n<TEXTOPEN_0001>stacks.project@gmail.com<TEXTCLOSE_0001>\nif you have a better suggestion.<FOOTNOTECLOSE_0001>\nif for every sheaf of sets <MATH_0003> we have <MATH_0004>\nfor all <MATH_0005>.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:58f616338b628d099fc8be055e944d557b05b685d73f69e91beae36a9bcd3cbb", "unit_id": "tag:072Y:statement"}
{"chapter": "sites-cohomology", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "079X", "placeholders": {"STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:32ce4e966c99c3475e1cbdb8fd848091ceebb243f9c0ef85306dc5f9e519738d", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nIt is clear that being totally acyclic is an intrinsic property, i.e.,\npreserved under equivalences of topoi.\nA totally acyclic sheaf has vanishing higher cohomology on all objects\nof the site, but in general the condition of being totally acyclic\nis strictly stronger.\nHere is a characterization of totally acyclic sheaves which is sometimes useful.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:b2778e5d21eee5b2586474f0858dd4e6393d4d1f5dc36f1cfd3e1033ff104b15", "unit_id": "tag:079X:prose-0003"}
